cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or refer to its findings, please cite the thesis below."
title: "On the Limits of Low-Frequency OHLCV Signals in Machine Learning-Driven Portfolio Optimization"
abstract: >-
  Source code and reproducible empirical framework for a Bachelor's thesis (Trabajo Fin de
  Grado) that stress-tests whether machine-learning models trained only on monthly features
  derived from daily OHLCV price-volume data can generate economically meaningful, net-of-cost
  portfolio improvements over disciplined baselines, on 30 large-cap US equities (2010-2025),
  using Black-Litterman allocation with Marchenko-Pastur covariance cleaning. The study
  documents a rigorous negative result: risk-adjusted performance is driven by covariance
  regularisation and Bayesian shrinkage rather than by the predictive content of the models.
type: software
